+# The Daily Grind
+
+A coffee shop site with passkey-only accounts. Requires Node.js 24+ and pnpm.
+
+## Setup
+
+Install dependencies with `pnpm install`, then run `pnpm build` to bundle the browser code. `pnpm start` serves both the page and the account API (default port 11222). The previous static-only server cannot verify passkeys; use the included application server when deploying this feature.
+
+For deployment, set:
+
+- `ORIGIN`: the exact public HTTPS origin, e.g. `https://coffee.example.com`, with no trailing slash.
+- `RP_ID`: the domain that owns the passkeys, e.g. `coffee.example.com`. Defaults to the hostname of `ORIGIN`. Keep this stable; changing it makes existing passkeys unusable on the new domain.
+- `PORT`: listening port, default `11222`.
+- `DATA_DIR`: persistent directory for SQLite, default `./data`. Mount persistent storage and back up this directory. Deploy one application instance; the request rate limiter is process-local.
+
+Terminate HTTPS at your reverse proxy and forward requests to this server. Use `http://localhost:11222` locally; it is the default origin. Cookies become Secure automatically with an HTTPS origin. The origin is configured explicitly rather than inferred from untrusted request headers. Do not serve the repository, database directory, or source files as a public static directory.
+
+## Account flows
+
+- **Registration:** choose a unique username, display name, and passkey label. The account is created only after WebAuthn attestation verification. Usernames are account identifiers, not verified email addresses.
+- **Authentication:** use the sign-in button or supported browser passkey autofill. Discoverable credentials allow username-free sign-in. Choosing another device or a security key is handled by the browser's native prompt.
+- **Management:** view creation and last-used dates, rename passkeys, and add/remove them. The last passkey cannot be removed. Add a backup on another device or in another password manager to avoid losing access. No password fallback or email recovery exists in this passkey-only application.
+- **Reauthentication:** identity verification requires user verification (PIN or biometrics), restricts credentials to the signed-in user, and unlocks account edits, passkey additions, and removals for five minutes. Primary sign-in and registration prefer user verification without rejecting authenticators that omit it.
+
+Only public keys and authenticator metadata are stored; private keys and biometric data stay with the authenticator. Sessions use random opaque HttpOnly cookies and expire after 24 hours. Authentication rotates the session ID. Challenges expire after five minutes, are bound to the session, user, ceremony, and verification level, and are consumed on every verification attempt. Mutating API requests require an exact matching Origin header. Credentials are verified with [SimpleWebAuthn](https://simplewebauthn.dev/docs/packages/server).
+
+The browser uses native WebAuthn APIs with bundled `webauthn-polyfills` for JSON serialization and capability discovery. Signal APIs are optional and feature-detected; failures do not undo completed operations. Common provider icons and names are display hints from the [passkey AAGUID registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids), with a generic fallback when metadata is unavailable. Provider identity never grants access.
+
+## Checks
+
+`pnpm check`, `pnpm test`, and `pnpm build` do not start a server. Tests invoke route handlers directly with an in-memory SQLite store and a software authenticator that creates real signed WebAuthn responses. They cover registration, sign-in, reauthentication, management, replay, ownership, origin, user-verification enforcement, and atomic persistence. `server.mjs` is the listening entrypoint; tests never import it.
